it‚dmarkxmhttps://grist.org/extreme-weather/wildfire-smoke-threatens-the-world-cup-final-fifa-still-doesnt-have-a-plan/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;mjU7c45-suqayE7J9DZcK2gIYIx_IU5ZK_9XsCMM8pAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hQBPN4y6x9hkU8fwCeEGrxaI18ukCimDMSZTE24_ag4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-17T15:55:16+00:00ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;95st7igdOuRrsGrvnrPIji2llCGowfIexdDu5L56QGsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxmhttps://grist.org/extreme-weather/wildfire-smoke-threatens-the-world-cup-final-fifa-still-doesnt-have-a-plan/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Y2KvHjmkjjZav8zNw23Q0fnMLEemAJCAmaWVn07XOOYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkf208531ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IdmzAMPWJBr0RtBmH4ZC7GWPr39fPKgSjuZl54EHSQcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;v2lYrQLamG6PTerVrnl9ziexv7_4p2P35RkpbDYrBrwƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;30SqdqjVGLm2NVOHGKXrJW4kCl8t_Hsc2pzChFcHQloƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxEpublic-access-observed; license-and-republication-rights-not-inferredƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;vFK_GWYhV48USqX8F6ElWv08VDlREy1aSYf9oigtPk0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyÔThe biggest game in soccer is set for Sunday, when Argentina and Spain will meet in the World Cup final, held just outside of New York City. Thereâ€™s just one problem â€” wildfire smoke now threatens the match and FIFA, the sportâ€™s governing body, doesnâ€™t appear to have any plan in place to protect player or fan health.

Wildfire smoke blowing from Canada has blanketed large swaths of the Midwest and East Coast in recent days, i mpacting more than a dozen states , with some of the worst-hit cities facing air quality in ranges considered hazardous. New Yorkers are among those who have been watching their air turn orange and hazy this week. On Thursday, the state issued an alert as the air quality index climbed to â€œunhealthyâ€ and â€œ very unhealthyâ€ levels. â€œAvoid spending time outdoors, if possible,â€ officials advised.

As of Friday morning, air quality near the stadium remained in the unhealthy range, and while itâ€™s forecast to improve to â€œmoderateâ€ by Sundayâ€™s kickoff, smoke patterns could still change. If concerns continue during the finals, thereâ€™s no way to close off the open-air stadium where stars Lionel Messi and Lamine Yamal are slated to square off. That means fans and players would almost certainly be exposed to smoke, which could lead to symptoms such as a burning throat, coughing, or headache, among others. This can be particularly dangerous for sensitive groups, including children, older individuals, and people with respiratory conditions such as asthma.

Players have also practiced outdoors during air quality events in the lead up to the game â€” the Spanish team held a practice Thursday in New Jersey. â€œThese are high-level athletes who are moving a lot of air through their lungs,â€ Dr. Courtney Howard, an emergency room physician and Global Climate and Health Alliance official, told the Associated Press . â€œThey shouldnâ€™t be practicing outside if the air quality levels are at hazardous sort of ranges for wildfire-related air pollution.â€

