â€œFIFA seems unprepared for these wildfires,â€ said Watanabe. â€œAt the same time, it looks like the worst of the pollution will dissipate before the World Cup Final, so FIFA will dodge a bullet and likely push forward with the match.â€

Some, however, question whether enough is being done, both by FIFA and other outdoor sports leagues facing these and other climate threats. FIFA has already been criticized this year for its policies regarding extreme heat .â€If we have to have a hydration break every 15 minutes, then we shouldnâ€™t be playing the game,â€ said Trinity Rodman , who played in the smoke-affected NWSL soccer game in New York.

One professional cyclist recently questioned whether the Tour de France should be held in the summer, as this yearâ€™s iteration of the iconic event has been plagued by wildfires, smoke, and extreme heat. â€œI would change the whole calendar,â€ said yellow jersey holder Tadej PogaÄar earlier this month. â€œItâ€™s not something I can do.â€

The power to make changes generally rests with a sportâ€™s governing body, rather than athletes, and Watanabe thinks climate issues should be taken more seriously. Even if the World Cup final is spared any impacts, he said this close call should serve as a warning.

â€œHopefully,â€ he said, â€œthis is a wakeup call to FIFA and all other sport leagues and competitions of the need for regulations and a comprehensive plan to deal with climate risks.â€

